DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Programs can use ppoossiixx__ffaaddvviissee() to announce an intention to access
       file data in a specific pattern in the future, thus allowing the kernel
       to perform appropriate optimizations.

       The _a_d_v_i_c_e applies to a (not necessarily existent) region starting at
       _o_f_f_s_e_t and extending for _s_i_z_e bytes (or until the end of the file if
       _s_i_z_e is 0) within the file referred to by _f_d.  The _a_d_v_i_c_e is not bind‐
       ing; it merely constitutes an expectation on behalf of the application.

       Permissible values for _a_d_v_i_c_e include:

       PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__NNOORRMMAALL
              Indicates  that  the application has no advice to give about its
              access pattern for the specified data.  If no  advice  is  given
              for an open file, this is the default assumption.

       PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__SSEEQQUUEENNTTIIAALL
              The  application  expects  to  access the specified data sequen‐
              tially (with lower offsets read before higher ones).

       PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__RRAANNDDOOMM
              The specified data will be accessed in random order.

       PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__NNOORREEUUSSEE
              The specified data will be accessed only once.

              Before Linux 2.6.18, PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__NNOORREEUUSSEE had the  same  semantics
              as  PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__WWIILLLLNNEEEEDD.   This  was  probably a bug; from Linux
              2.6.18 until Linux 6.2 this flag was a no‐op.  Since Linux  6.3,
              PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__NNOORREEUUSSEE  signals that the kernel page replacement al‐
              gorithm can ignore access to mapped page cache  marked  by  this
              flag.  This is useful, for example, while streaming large files.

       PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__WWIILLLLNNEEEEDD
              The specified data will be accessed in the near future.

              PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__WWIILLLLNNEEEEDD  initiates  a nonblocking read of the speci‐
              fied region into the page cache.  The amount of data read may be
              decreased by the kernel depending on virtual  memory  load.   (A
              few  megabytes  will  usually  be  fully  satisfied, and more is
              rarely useful.)

       PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__DDOONNTTNNEEEEDD
              The specified data will not be accessed in the near future.

              PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__DDOONNTTNNEEEEDD attempts to  free  cached  pages  associated
              with  the  specified region.  This is useful, for example, while
              streaming large files.  A program may periodically  request  the
              kernel  to  free cached data that has already been used, so that
              more useful cached pages are not discarded instead.

              Requests to discard partial pages are ignored.  It is preferable
              to preserve needed data than discard unneeded data.  If the  ap‐
              plication  requires that data be considered for discarding, then
              _o_f_f_s_e_t and _s_i_z_e must be page‐aligned.

              The implementation _m_a_y attempt to write back dirty pages in  the
              specified  region,  but  this  is not guaranteed.  Any unwritten
              dirty pages will not be freed.  If the application wishes to en‐
              sure that dirty pages will be released, it should call  ffssyynncc(2)
              or ffddaattaassyynncc(2) first.

