VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       Under Linux, PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__NNOORRMMAALL sets the readahead window to the default
       size for the backing device; PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__SSEEQQUUEENNTTIIAALL doubles  this  size,
       and     PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__RRAANNDDOOMM    disables    file    readahead    entirely.
       PPOOSSIIXX__FFAADDVV__NNOORREEUUSSEE does not modify the readahead  window  size.   These
       changes  affect  the  entire  file,  not just the specified region (but
       other open file handles to the same file are unaffected).

   CC lliibbrraarryy//kkeerrnneell ddiiffffeerreenncceess
       The name of the wrapper function in the C library  is  ppoossiixx__ffaaddvviissee().
       The underlying system call is called ffaaddvviissee6644() (or, on some architec‐
       tures, ffaaddvviissee6644__6644()); the difference between the two is that the for‐
       mer  system  call assumes that the type of the _s_i_z_e argument is _s_i_z_e___t,
       while the latter expects _l_o_f_f___t there.

   AArrcchhiitteeccttuurree‐‐ssppeecciiffiicc vvaarriiaannttss
       Some architectures require 64‐bit arguments to be aligned in a suitable
       pair of registers (see ssyyssccaallll(2) for further detail).  On such  archi‐
       tectures,  the  call signature of ppoossiixx__ffaaddvviissee() shown in the SYNOPSIS
       would force a register to be wasted as padding between the _f_d and  _o_f_f_‐
       _s_e_t  arguments.  Therefore, these architectures define a version of the
       system call that orders the arguments suitably, but  is  otherwise  ex‐
       actly the same as ppoossiixx__ffaaddvviissee().

       For example, since Linux 2.6.14, ARM has the following system call:

           lloonngg aarrmm__ffaaddvviissee6644__6644((iinntt _f_d,, iinntt _a_d_v_i_c_e,,
                                 llooffff__tt _o_f_f_s_e_t,, llooffff__tt _s_i_z_e));;

       These  architecture‐specific details are generally hidden from applica‐
       tions by the glibc ppoossiixx__ffaaddvviissee() wrapper function, which invokes  the
       appropriate architecture‐specific system call.

